6045417] [17592186161219 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2fb9df63-3e91-5305-ab66-7239c594b50a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161220 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161220 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161220 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "463dc66e-e8fb-5326-b371-3f4a5d8ec9cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161221 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "698774d3-bbc4-5952-8a59-a504e856805c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161797 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161797 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+x_2^2+2x_3^2+4x_1x_2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161797 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce37b130-8aa9-58af-b3b8-bf78b5a741e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161797 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/09/09d935cd479371e7cde4e52b2073cb23343a5137f311803c8da5879a59896fb4.svg)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/51/51694be12d594a39b6a07f48b37aedb47323fc5537a60f5d6a766576103d48f6.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161798 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161798 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2-2x_1x_2+2x_2^2,$ find its canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161798 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8ec4ba0-e0bf-5842-8f5e-4914ce46706a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161798 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/69/69dcf821f3ff202b567b901d8f512c5cecf7159617ca51a0664c14a5099e9715.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/fc/fc9465d4e5bcd43a3c29e5946b05ec18cdbc7453a4011aa548f374016c561b45.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/59/59c1bd46325fab8a52fed162d7ae47e8fb33a10cc524cd770a3b18f5934e51dd.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/ad/adc9241219f3b5ebce0d8418bf34e250b7230ea612fabca5bdfc109c98a18dbb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/dc/dcab437eb77b29ab5bcd5c3ae14b77f10f01382aafadf2aecded1bb1fb72dc32.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/3f/3f6b1002893290dde6c9a68425b0035e249ff3630a41d29c9da5756c1a18f6a6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186161799 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161799 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/61/6135fe578745ebf2eccb4370557f0dba6ec27361a192e4e87536eadad87a39bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ to the canonical (diagonal) form $\\mathbf{y}^T \\! D \\mathbf{y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161799 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "243d1a75-6f0f-5d55-a7ec-333487fa37bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161799 #edb/ref 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matr