ratic Form Using Orthogonal Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162221 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7a60cb12-dd77-5740-b1c2-9fd58434b948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186356961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162631 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162631 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162631 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162631 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72599bcc-35e4-5761-98ef-23a9eae51976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162631 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162632 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162632 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162632 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162632 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1587eada-a2ce-573f-9e77-1eddebc42115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162632 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162653 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162653 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95064" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162653 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162653 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162653 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14118443-b386-51d2-bcd8-a6f6bd250385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162653 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162654 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162654 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162654 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00c817da-6580-5c3d-a2f1-9c059dd42da6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162654 #edb/ref 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1061 30542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a819afd-fa45-5c12-8319-bbdf2452f54b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162854 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1061 30849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1325a77d-0093-5d23-9495-014e8f550773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1061 30850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "459f8fc0-d5b4-5716-9c7e-669c15d5ffdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162894 #edb/ref 1061 30851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65c62c86-c42d-58f2-9237-353231d4f733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a quadratic form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal. \n\nNow, substituting the change of variable $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}$ into our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}\\,AP)y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,Dy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form. \n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A,$ we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/43/43e0dc28161c0564a75f6ea2dc7f0e09ad74dffae05b864e13c61330221f2983.svg)\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/c9/c93cabd6a18df429b5aad99815b23e337c17b88a1c271dd17344631dfc5f8ebb.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1065 4888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "26652f7d-7ad4-54a0-b4a6-5d9faafa8403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163229 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269433 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12bf9c48-adf5-5505-9acf-470bcd00e2e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269634 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269634 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269634 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28ba23a7-0de5-509b-82c4-df9110368ebb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269675 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269675 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269675 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d5b8660-6902-5581-9ba0-3793ddcec3a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271301 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271301 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271301 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8c8a43e-5e4e-5fb1-9edb-1b9646ab1963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271374 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186271374 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271374 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "efaafb17-4207-5a9d-9693-07fa68e3b409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186271301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271724 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "787c368e-889d-5b8c-b239-21ca9558eae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272181 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140998" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272181 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272181 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b8238cd-632f-5ae3-b721-832a7f9669c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272181 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\sqrt{1}0}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354677 #edb/ref 1080 "2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354677 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354677 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0254e5ff-cb6e-59c0-96cb-5313827be000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354730 #edb/ref 1080 "{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3